<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<UserControl
    x:Class="MeetingTranscriber.App.ChannelMeter"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:local="using:MeetingTranscriber.App"
    xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
    xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
    mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!-- The one component that is this application's own, drawn from `docs/design.md` §The meter.
         One control and not one per channel: two meters is the contract — channel 0 is the others
         and channel 1 is you — and anything the same on two screens is one component before it is
         on the second one. It is on five of the eighteen artboards.

         It shows now and never a history. A strip of the last few seconds answers for the process
         before this one, which is the wrong answer at the moment it is read.

         No literal and no value chosen on the screen: every colour is a brush key and every text is
         a rank from Olivo. The five numbers that are this component's own anatomy rather than the
         system's — the bar's 20 high, the scale's 18, the segment's 3 on and 3 off, the peak's 2
         wide and 4 proud — are in ChannelMeter.xaml.cs beside the code that lays them out, because
         a token used once is a token that only moves the value somewhere harder to find.

         Square ends, and the four-value radius scale deliberately does not reach here: a quantity
         drawn as a length is not a card, and a rounded cap on a bar that is twenty pixels tall is a
         cap that lies about where the level is.

         What it does **not** carry is the three states — being heard, clipping, no signal — and the
         fourth condition that is not a state, a source that died. Those are words and a dimming
         that answer *what is wrong with this channel*, which is what cards #101 and #102 are for. A
         slot here for a caller that does not exist yet would cost more than the line it saved. -->

    <Grid RowSpacing="10">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- Nothing here says which channel this is, and that is the redraw rather than an
             omission. `docs/design.md` §Where it goes pins the meter to the control that chooses
             its source, so the mono chip, the role and the picker are the row above and belong to
             `ChannelStrip` — a meter that wrote *Canal 0 · los demás* over its own bar as well
             would be one column saying the same thing twice. What stays here is what nothing else
             in this application draws: the four layers, the scale under them and the retained
             peak. The name a screen reader reads off the bar is still set from outside, because the
             bar is the thing carrying a value. -->

        <StackPanel Grid.Row="0" Spacing="6">
            <!-- The four layers, bottom to top, all on the same segments. Each is that one set of
                 segments clipped to its own range and never a rectangle sized to it: a sized layer
                 re-tiles its own pattern and the segments walk as the level moves.

                 This replaced a ProgressBar, which could not be it: one rounded fill, no hot zone,
                 no scale and no peak, so nothing on it said whether −16 was close to clipping or
                 nowhere near it. -->
            <Grid x:Name="Bar" Height="20" SizeChanged="OnBarResized">
                <Canvas x:Name="TrackLayer" />
                <Canvas x:Name="HotZoneLayer" />
                <Canvas x:Name="LevelLayer" />
                <Canvas x:Name="ClippedLayer" />

                <!-- The meter's only memory. It stands where the loudest moment was and does not
                     decay at all, which is why it needs no rate where the level needs one. -->
                <Rectangle
                    x:Name="RetainedPeak"
                    Width="2"
                    Margin="0,-4,0,-4"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    Fill="{StaticResource PeakBrush}"
                    Visibility="Collapsed" />
            </Grid>

            <!-- The zero is always there. Without the scale under it the bars are decoration:
                 nothing says whether −16 is close to clipping or nowhere near it. Laid out on a
                 Canvas because each number sits at its own fraction of the bar — 0, 33.3, 66.7, 80
                 and 100 per cent — and a Grid cannot put one at 66.7. -->
            <Canvas x:Name="ScaleUnderTheBar" Height="18" SizeChanged="OnScaleResized" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- How loud it is now, and the loudest it has been. Both are numbers that get compared to
             another one, so both are mono at the data rank — the level in tinta because it is what
             the row is about, the peak in pico because it is the peak.

             The artboard sets the level at 14/18 mono 500, which is an eighth rank: the ramp has
             seven and its only mono entries are the stopwatch at 62/72 and data at 11/18.
             `docs/design.md` §The artboards and this page says the page is what gets built, and it
             answers this case itself — a second line that is quieter is quieter by colour, and
             there is no rank between Body and Data to reach for. So the two are one rank in two
             inks. The artboard is what gets corrected. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="1">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <TextBlock
                x:Name="Level"
                Grid.Column="0"
                Style="{StaticResource DataText}"
                Foreground="{StaticResource InkBrush}" />

            <TextBlock
                x:Name="Peak"
                Grid.Column="1"
                Style="{StaticResource DataText}"
                Foreground="{StaticResource PeakBrush}" />
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
